T}@T{
T}@T{
T}
.TE
.PP
The x86_64 timings are from an Intel i7-12800H performance core; the
aarch64 timings are from an AWS t4g (Graviton2) instance running Alpine
Linux.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]-O2\f[R], the default, compiles about 30% slower than \f[B]-O1\f[R]
and runs 10\[en]16% faster, which a \f[I]cdef\f[R] called more than a
handful of times pays back.
\f[B]-O3\f[R] costs more compile time for no measurable gain in typical
code.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]-O0\f[R] or \f[B]-O1\f[R] suit code that runs only once or a few
times, such as one-off generated code where compile time dominates.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]-g\f[R] without an explicit \f[B]-O\f[R] gives full variable
inspection in gdb, by keeping every local variable in memory and
disabling inlining.
That makes the code several times slower (4.5× in the table above), so
it is for interactive debugging, not production.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]-g -O2\f[R], or \f[B]-g\f[R] with any explicit level from
\f[B]-O1\f[R] up, runs at full speed.
The debug information costs compile time but nothing at run time.
It keeps backtraces, line numbers and call frames, live in gdb or from a
core file, but some locals can\[cq]t be printed, as with gcc\[cq]s
\f[B]-g -O2\f[R].
This is the form to use for production code that wants debug
information.
.IP \[bu] 2
Calls between functions in a \f[I]cdef\f[R] are cheap.
Moving the lexer\[cq]s rule actions above into small helper functions
costs under 1%, and calling them through function pointers about 2%.
.IP \[bu] 2
MIR inlines calls to small functions (up to about 50 MIR instructions; a
helper like \f[B]replace_tclobj\f[R] is 37) defined in the same
\f[I]cdef\f[R], wherever they are called.
Inlining a helper removes the call and, often more importantly, lets its
body be optimized together with the caller\[cq]s code.
A function making 48 \f[B]replace_tclobj\f[R] calls in a loop runs about
3.6× faster with them inlined than as calls.
Inlining grows the caller, and so its compile time, by roughly 4 ms per
1000 instructions added at \f[B]-O2\f[R] on x86_64.
As a circuit breaker, each function stops inlining once it has grown by
about 2000 instructions (and by 50% of its original size), so a function
making hundreds of helper calls has only the first few dozen inlined.
.IP \[bu] 2
Functions declared \f[B]inline\f[R] (such as \f[B]static inline\f[R]
helpers) may be larger: calls to them are inlined for bodies of up to
about 200 MIR instructions, as long as the caller hasn\[cq]t grown by
50% through inlining.
\f[B]inline\f[R] is only a hint, as in any C compiler, but it\[cq]s the
way to ask for a mid-sized helper to be inlined.
.IP \[bu] 2
Calls through function pointers, to Tcl\[cq]s C API, to other
\f[I]cdef\f[R]s (\f[B]use\f[R], \f[B]symbols\f[R]) or to
\f[B]library\f[R] code are never inlined.
When \f[B]-g\f[R] is given without \f[B]-O\f[R], nothing is inlined.
.SS BUGS
.PP
Please report any bugs to the github issue tracker:
https://github.com/cyanogilvie/jitc/issues
.SS SEE ALSO
.PP
critcl: https://wiki.tcl-lang.org/page/Critcl, tcc4tcl:
https://wiki.tcl-lang.org/page/tcc4tcl, slimcc:
https://github.com/fuhsnn/slimcc (libslimcc fork:
https://github.com/cyanogilvie/slimcc), MIR:
https://github.com/vnmakarov/mir, re2c:
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Re2c, packcc:
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PackCC, lemon:
https://sqlite.org/src/doc/trunk/doc/lemon.html.
.SS PROJECT STATUS
.PP
Versions up to 0.7 were in heavy production use with an embedded TinyCC
compiler.
0.8.0 replaces it with libslimcc and MIR: C23 with \f[B]defer\f[R], an
optimizing code generator, multiple architectures (x86_64, aarch64), and
no temporary shared objects or \f[B]dlopen\f[R].
.SS BUILDING
.PP
There are no external dependencies other than Tcl.
The libslimcc and MIR backends are built as meson subprojects, fetched
from their pinned git commits during \f[V]meson setup\f[R] (so the first
setup needs network access).
Build from the release tarball:
https://github.com/cyanogilvie/jitc/releases/download/v0.8.1/jitc-v0.8.1.tar.gz
or recursively clone the git repo:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/cyanogilvie/jitc
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Building uses meson:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
meson setup build --buildtype=release
meson compile -C build
meson test -C build
meson install -C build
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Both Tcl 8.6 and 9.0 are supported.
To build against a specific Tcl installation, set
\f[V]PKG_CONFIG_PATH\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/path/to/tcl/lib/pkgconfig meson setup build
\f[R]
.fi
